    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Formed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>, it stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>.</li><li>It held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s only Iberian legion, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-vii-gemina">VII Gemina</a> at León, guarding the great gold mines of the north-west.</li><li>Split by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> around 293, the reduced province was the last Roman territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in the 470s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> told Augustus that a palm tree had sprouted on his altar, he reportedly replied that this showed how often they lit fires on it, according to Quintilian.</p></aside>
    </section>

