    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa.jpg" alt="Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrippa_Gabii_Louvre_Ma1208.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Tarraconensis grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>, ‘Nearer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’, one of the two provinces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> created in 197 BCE from its conquests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> after the Second Punic War. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> itself had been the base of the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-cornelius-scipio-consul-218-bce">Publius Cornelius Scipio</a> from 218 BCE, and over the next two centuries Roman control spread inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast through the Celtiberian Wars, which ended with the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> in 133 BCE. Augustus took the province under his own control in the settlement of 27 BCE and directed the final conquest of the north. In 26–25 BCE he campaigned in person against the Cantabri and Astures but fell ill and withdrew to Tarraco, where he spent the winter and received foreign embassies, so that for a time the city was the effective centre of the empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars">Cantabrian Wars</a> were finished by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Agrippa</a> in 19 BCE. The newly conquered lands of Gallaecia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a> were attached to Citerior, probably during Augustus' visit of 16–13 BCE, creating a vast province that stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the Mediterranean coast to the Atlantic shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>. Named after its capital, it was one of the largest provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tarraco"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarraco.jpg" alt="Tarraco" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarragona_-_Amfiteatre_04_2016-08-31.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rafa Esteve, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tarraconensis contained great contrasts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley had felt Greek, Phoenician and Iberian influence for centuries and became thoroughly Roman, with cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>), Emporiae, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valentia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Caesaraugusta</a> (Zaragoza). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta</a> was the land of the Celtiberians and other Celtic-speaking peoples, and the north-west remained a region of hillforts (<em>castros</em>) and dispersed settlement long after the conquest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> counted 179 towns in the province besides many smaller communities. Its economy was among the richest in the West. The coast around Barcino exported wine in large quantities in the first century, mostly in locally made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a>, while Carthago Nova was famed for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, its esparto grass and its fish sauce. The gold mines of the north-west, worked on a vast scale by hydraulic methods at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/las-medulas">Las Médulas</a> and other sites, were among the empire's main sources of gold; Pliny reports that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturia</a>, Gallaecia and Lusitania together yielded 20,000 Roman pounds a year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> culture flourished: the rhetorician Quintilian came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calahorra">Calagurris</a> (Calahorra) and the poet Martial from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbilis">Bilbilis</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was established by the mid-third century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuosus-of-tarragona">Fructuosus of Tarraco</a> was burned in the city's amphitheatre in 259.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/strabo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strabo.jpg" alt="Strabo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strabo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">André Thévet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tarraconensis was the only Iberian province with a permanent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> garrison. Augustus' conquest of the north required perhaps seven legions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, writing under Augustus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, describes three legions stationed in the province. Their number fell as the north-west was pacified, and from the reign of Vespasian a single legion, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-vii-gemina">Legio VII Gemina</a>, raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a> in 68, held a camp in the north-west that grew into the city of León, whose name derives from <em>legio</em>. With its auxiliary units it guarded the gold-mining districts and supervised the region's roads. The province also supplied soldiers for service elsewhere, and Hispanic auxiliary units served on many frontiers. Imperial peace was broken in the third century. According to later writers, Frankish raiders crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> in the 260s, damaging the city, and in the late third and fourth centuries many towns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lucus Augusti</a> (Lugo), Legio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a>, built strong walls; the walls of Lugo, over two kilometres long, still stand complete. In the fifth century the province's defence depended increasingly on Roman generals sent from Gaul and on Visigothic allies, while the Legio VII Gemina disappears from the record and local forces proved unable to stop either invaders or rebels.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tacitus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacitus.jpg" alt="Tacitus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-_Parlament-Tacitus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pe-Jo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As one of the emperor's provinces, Tarraconensis was governed by a <em>legatus Augusti pro praetore</em> of consular rank, one of the most senior posts in the imperial service. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes how the governor was assisted by subordinate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a>, one commanding the legions in the north-west and others administering justice, while a procurator managed imperial finances and the mines. The province was divided into seven judicial districts (<em>conventus</em>), centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Caesaraugusta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clunia">Clunia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturica-augusta">Asturica Augusta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lucus Augusti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a>. Tarraco was the seat of the provincial council, which met to honour the emperor; in 15 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> allowed the province to build a temple to the deified Augustus there, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says set an example for all the provinces, and the upper city was later rebuilt as a monumental complex for the provincial cult and council. Its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Servius Sulpicius Galba</a> was proclaimed emperor at Carthago Nova in 68, and Hadrian held an assembly of the Hispanic provinces at Tarraco in the winter of 122–123. Vespasian's grant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> around 73–74 turned hundreds of communities into self-governing municipalities. Around 293 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> divided the province, creating Gallaecia in the north-west and Carthaginiensis in the south and centre, and leaving a smaller Tarraconensis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley and the north-east.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tower-of-hercules"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tower-of-hercules.jpg" alt="Tower of Hercules" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tower-of-hercules">Tower of Hercules</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torre_de_H%C3%A9rcules_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fernando, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tarraconensis was the main channel through which Roman civilisation reached most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Its roads, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-augusta">Via Augusta</a>, which ran down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, and the network linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, shaped the routes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> for centuries. Its colonies and municipalities, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Caesaraugusta</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toletum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a>, became the cities of medieval and modern Spain and Portugal, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> spoken in the province developed into Catalan, Aragonese, Castilian, Galician and Portuguese. Roman engineering left lasting monuments: the aqueduct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tower-of-hercules">Tower of Hercules</a> lighthouse at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-coruna">A Coruña</a>, still in use, the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a>, the gold-mining landscape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/las-medulas">Las Médulas</a> and the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> are all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. The province also produced writers who shaped Latin literature, including Quintilian, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institutio-oratoria">Institutio Oratoria</a></em> became a standard work on education, Martial, the master of the epigram, and, in the fourth century, the Christian poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prudentius">Prudentius</a>, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calahorra">Calagurris</a>. The church of Tarraco became a leading metropolitan see; the oldest surviving papal decretal, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-siricius">Pope Siricius</a> in 385, was addressed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Himerius of Tarraco. The ecclesiastical province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarragona">Tarragona</a> still exists today.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilerda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilerda.jpg" alt="Battle of Ilerda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilerda">Battle of Ilerda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Illerda,_map_1_(Caesar%CB%90_A_History_Of_The_Art_Of_War_Among_The_Romans_Down_To_The_End,_vol._II,_1892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Theodore Ayrault Dodge, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The reduced province of Tarraconensis created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> remained under Roman control longer than any other part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Alans and Suebi broke into the peninsula in 409, they settled in the west and south, and the Roman government held the north-east as its base, using Visigothic allies to fight the invaders in 416–418. In the 440s and 450s, however, the province was shaken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagaudae">Bagaudae</a>, rebels drawn from impoverished peasants and others outside imperial control. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-asturius">Asturius</a> in 441 and his son-in-law, the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merobaudes-poet">Merobaudes</a>, in 443 were sent against them, but in 449 a Bagaudic leader, Basilius, killed federate troops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarazona">Turiasso</a> (Tarazona) in his church, then joined the Suebic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rechiar">Rechiar</a> in plundering the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Caesaraugusta</a> and seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilerda">Ilerda</a>. In 456 the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii">Theodoric II</a>, acting in the name of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>, crushed the Suebi, and Visigothic power in Hispania grew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a>, king from 466, broke openly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>: his commanders captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> and Caesaraugusta, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> and the coastal cities, probably around 472–475. With the fall of Tarraco Roman rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> ended, though the province's name, towns and church structures survived within the Visigothic Kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Tarraconensis, the largest Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> for some five centuries, guarded the gold mines of the north-west with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s only Iberian legion, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature Quintilian and Martial, and was the last part of Hispania to remain under Roman rule before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> took it.</em></p>

